      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22687"
      cmd         : "PDIR=$(/bin/mysql -Nse 'SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES;' 2>/dev/null | /bin/grep '^plugin_dir' | /bin/awk '{print $2}'); PMODE=$(/bin/stat -c '%a' $PDIR 2>/dev/null); POWN=$(/bin/stat -c '%U' $PDIR 2>/dev/null); PGRP=$(/bin/stat -c '%G' $PDIR 2>/dev/null); if ! /bin/echo $PDIR | /bin/grep -q .; then /bin/echo FAIL: plugin_dir not reported by the server; elif ! /bin/echo $PMODE | /bin/grep -q .; then /bin/echo FAIL: plugin_dir $PDIR does not exist or is unreadable; elif [ $POWN = mysql ] && [ $PGRP = mysql ] && { [ $PMODE -eq 550 ] || [ $PMODE -eq 554 ]; }; then /bin/echo PASS: plugin_dir $PDIR mode=$PMODE owner=$POWN group=$PGRP; else WRIT=$(/bin/find $PDIR -maxdepth 1 -perm /022 2>/dev/null); /bin/echo FAIL: plugin_dir $PDIR has mode=$PMODE owner=$POWN group=$PGRP - required mode 550 or 554 with owner mysql and group mysql - remediate with chmod 550 $PDIR and chown mysql:mysql $PDIR - group or world writable entries: $WRIT; fi"
      expect      : "^PASS:"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "Check audit_log_file is configured"
          cmd         : "/bin/mysql -Nse 'SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES;' 2>/dev/null | /bin/grep '^audit_log_file[^_]' | /bin/grep -c '.'"
          expect      : "^[1-9]"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CHECK
          description : "3.9 Ensure 'audit_log_file' Has Appropriate Permissions"
          info        : "MySQL can operate using a variety of log files, each used for different purposes. These are the binary log, error log, slow query log, relay log, audit log, and general log. Because these are files on the host operating system, they are subject to the permissions and ownership structure provided by the host and may be accessible by users other than the MySQL user.

Limiting the accessibility of these objects will protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the MySQL logs."
          solution    : "Execute the following commands for the audit_log_file discovered in the audit procedure:

chmod 660 <audit_log_file>
chown mysql:mysql <audit_log_file>

Impact:

Changing the permissions and ownership of the audit log file may have an impact on who can access and edit the audit log. Such changes can affect monitoring tools which may be using a log file adapter or scripted alternatives. Also, the audit log may be used for alerting by infrastructure teams which can affect real-time audit capability."
